<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>A contour survey in Adelaide is the measured picture of your block that plans get drawn on. It shows where the house, sheds, trees, fences and services sit, and how high or low the ground is at each point. Your designer, builder or council usually asks for one before a Development Application goes through PlanSA, and the request might say feature survey, level survey, contour survey or detail survey.</p><p>Those names describe one family of survey, and the rest of this page explains how they overlap. Torrens Survey Co is an advertising and introduction service. It doesn't survey and holds no surveying licence; it puts you in touch with an independent Licensed Surveyor, who quotes and contracts directly with you. Call (08) 5550 3814 with the request from your designer in hand and say what you're trying to build.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Handle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">What's Included in Every Visit</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 8px;">Existing features located</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Buildings, fences, walls, paving, driveways, trees and visible services are picked up and drawn to scale. Your designer can see what's staying, what's going and what's in the way.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 8px;">Levels and contours</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Ground levels are measured at points across the block and joined into contour lines. The fall of the land becomes readable on one sheet.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 8px;">Levels tied to a datum</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Heights are referenced to a known datum, so your designer, builder and council are all reading the same numbers. Ask the surveyor which datum the job will use.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 8px;">Neighbouring features</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Where they matter to your design, things like a boundary wall or a neighbour's floor level can be picked up as far as they are visible from the site. Say up front which ones you care about.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 8px;">A file your designer can use</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The result is normally a scaled drawing plus a digital file the designer can build plans on. Confirm the format with your designer before you accept a quote.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 8px;">A written scope</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The area covered, the features picked up, the contour detail, the datum and the file format should all be on the quote. That's what lets you compare two quotes properly.</p>
      </div>
</div>
