<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Feature, level, contour and detail: one family, four names</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>The words overlap because different professions use different ones. A <strong>feature survey</strong> locates the things on the block. A <strong>level survey</strong> measures heights. A <strong>contour survey</strong> turns those heights into contour lines showing the shape of the ground. A <strong>detail survey</strong> is the older term for much the same combined job.</p><p>In practice most residential jobs ask for all of it in one drawing, which is why a good quote says what's included instead of relying on the label. Before you sign off on a quote, check these:</p><ul><li>Which features are picked up: buildings, fences, walls, trees, paving, visible services.</li><li>How the levels are shown: spot levels, contour lines, or both.</li><li>Which datum the heights are tied to.</li><li>Whether neighbouring features are included.</li></ul><p>This isn't the same as a <a href="/boundary-surveys/">boundary survey</a>. If the position of the boundary matters to your project, see the boundary section further down.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Why levels decide drainage and retaining wall costs</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Water moves downhill and building work has to work with it. The fall across your block decides where stormwater can go, whether a slab can sit flat or has to step, and how much soil has to be cut away or built up. Every metre of height difference you have to hold back becomes a retaining wall conversation, and retaining walls are one of the items that can move a building budget.</p><p>That's why the levels matter more than the pretty drawing. A designer working from guessed heights can draw a house that looks fine on paper and then needs earthworks nobody priced. A level survey done before the design is settled lets the designer place the floor level, driveway grade and drainage falls against real ground heights. Ask your designer or builder what they need the levels for, and pass that on when you ask for a quote.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What the plan must show and where a survey fits</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>The Planning, Development and Infrastructure (General) Regulations 2017, Schedule 8, set out what a Development Application for a new building, structure or extension must include. That list includes a site plan drawn to scale showing the boundaries and dimensions of the site, the distance from any proposed building to the front, side and rear boundaries, and existing ground and floor levels where relevant. It also asks for proposed finished floor levels and site levels, including in relation to the top of any kerb, and the height and location of any earthworks or retaining walls.</p><p>The regulation doesn't say who must prepare that site plan. A feature and level survey is the usual way to get the existing levels and offsets it needs, so designers commonly ask for one. Your designer or the council can tell you whether your project needs it.</p><p>A feature and level survey isn't a substitute for finding the boundary. A fence line, a title plan, online mapping or an aerial photo doesn't show the legal boundary. In South Australia, only licensed surveyors can undertake a boundary survey or place a survey mark that defines a property boundary, according to the Surveyors Board of South Australia. If you're building close to a boundary, ask about an <a href="/identification-surveys/">identification survey</a> or a boundary survey alongside the feature and level work, and never move or remove a survey mark.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What to put in your brief before you ask for a quote</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Surveyors quote each job, and the price depends on the block, the evidence on site and the type of survey. A clear brief gets a quote you can compare. Give the surveyor:</p><ul><li>The address and, if you have it, the Certificate of Title.</li><li>What you are planning: extension, new home, shed, pool, driveway or a block split (see <a href="/land-division-surveys/">land division surveys</a>).</li><li>How much of the block needs covering, and whether the street frontage and kerb are included.</li><li>Whether neighbouring features, trees or services matter to the design.</li><li>The file format your designer wants.</li><li>Whether you also need the boundary position identified.</li></ul><p>It's also fair to ask how the surveyor will pick up your site. Total stations, satellite (GNSS) receivers, laser scanning and drone photogrammetry all suit different blocks, and dense tree cover or tight side passages can make some methods harder. The surveyor should tell you what they plan to use and why.</p><p>No turnaround time is published here, because none is sourced. The Licensed Surveyor tells you when the drawing can be delivered when they quote. For a rough idea of budgets, see <a href="/land-surveying-cost-adelaide/">land surveying cost in Adelaide</a>, and call (08) 5550 3814 if you're not sure what to ask for.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EEF3F1;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">Is a contour survey the same as a feature and level survey?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Mostly yes. Feature, level, contour and detail surveys are one family, and most residential jobs combine them in a single drawing showing existing features, spot levels and contour lines. Different designers and councils use different names, so ask the surveyor to list what is included on the quote rather than relying on the label.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does a contour survey cost in Adelaide?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">No Adelaide firm we checked publishes a price for this survey, so there's no Adelaide figure to quote. One national guide (Trade Heroes, updated 6 March 2026) gives $800 to $2,200 for a feature and level survey and $1,200 to $3,000 for a contour survey, GST not stated, and other national guides run higher, up to $4,500. Treat those as Australia-wide background only, because the Licensed Surveyor quotes after seeing your block.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I need a feature and level survey for a Development Application?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Schedule 8 of the General Regulations 2017 requires a scaled site plan showing boundaries, distances to boundaries and existing and proposed levels, but it doesn't say who prepares that plan. A feature and level survey is the usual way to get the existing levels and offsets. Ask your designer or the council what your application needs.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">Does a feature and level survey show where my boundary is?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Not by itself. The boundary can only be determined by a Licensed Surveyor, who physically marks it, and a fence line or online map doesn't show it. To check that a surveyor is licensed in South Australia, contact the Registrar on registrar@isasa.org.au or (08) 8212 0343, or use the Surveyors Board's public register.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E4DFD4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I need contours if I'm dividing my block?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">It depends on the division. Where land is divided into more than 5 allotments or a new road is involved, the plan must show the contours of the present surface of the ground above a known datum and be vouched for by a licensed surveyor. Simple divisions that are deemed-to-satisfy need only a final plan, and your Licensed Surveyor or the council can confirm which applies.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
